#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build the host-side Platinum asset pack straight off a guest disk image.

No VM, no wire, no guest. The route — and the two steps in it that read
as dead ends — is written up in docs/asset-extraction-offline.md:

  1. qemu-img converts the qcow2 to raw. That is a FILE CONVERTER, not the
     emulator; nothing boots and the source image is only ever read.
  2. Block 0 is an Apple Partition Map; take the Apple_HFS entry.
  3. That partition is an HFS *wrapper* (signature BD). Modern macOS
     dropped HFS Standard, so `hdiutil` refuses it and the route looks
     impossible. The real HFS+ volume is EMBEDDED inside; the MDB says
     where. Carve it to its own file and macOS mounts it normally.
  4. Resource forks are then ordinary files at <path>/..namedfork/rsrc.

The parsers are the ones the live-pull extractor already uses
(mirror/tools/extract-assets); this script only replaces the transport,
so the two routes cannot disagree about what an icl8 means.

    tools/extract-assets-offline                       # default image + pack
    tools/extract-assets-offline --image X.qcow2       # another image
    tools/extract-assets-offline --reuse-work          # skip convert+carve
    tools/extract-assets-offline --theme-report        # census the theme file
    tools/extract-assets-offline --accent-ramps        # print the 21 clut ramps

RULES, not preferences. These are Apple's bitmaps. The pack stays in this
private repo, never publishes, never ships as an artifact. The source
image is opened READ-ONLY and every step operates on the converted COPY;
nothing is ever written into a guest System Folder.
"""

